Z||000000||Cozmo||10-14-2001||07:30 PM||||I want to keep a "Pontiac" look, so I've modified a steering wheel out of a newer Bonneville that has radio controls to fit on a Fiero steering column.<BR>I pretty much am stuck with the cassette deck that came with it and was wondering what I could do to make it sound better without compromising that same "Pontiac" look.<BR>The system is going into my '85 2M6 Targa (no sail panel speaker cutouts) and I am eliminating the seat speakers.<BR>I have a set of Delco/Bose door mount component speakers (they even have a nameplate that says "performance sound" in the same style as the Fiero subwoofer control) and some 6x9s that came from the same car.<BR>I was thinking of putting the 6x9s in the dash as the front channels and using the door speakers as the rear channels.  I also have a factory sub enclosure to add into the mix (oooh... bad pun... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<BR>I was contemplating using a 4ch power amp for the front and rears driven by the stock deck and a separate amp for the subbie with an upgraded driver.<BR>Problem is, the car sits around a lot with the top off and I don't want the stereo upgrades to be too obvious. <BR>Has anyone done any "stealth" stereo upgrades? This is a long term project and will probably go through a few design iterations before I'm satisfied with it.<BR>I know this subject has been discussed a lot, anyone have any ideas or suggestions?<P>BobC <BR>  ||||reg||5
